    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>No randomised trial has tested whether an oral collagen supplement tightens loose skin after weight loss, so no brand can honestly promise it.</strong> Skin biopsied after major weight loss shows disorganised collagen and fragmented elastic fibres, and mature elastin has an estimated half-life near 70 years, so adult skin barely rebuilds it.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The evidence gap:</strong> collagen trials measure hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth, never excess skin.</li>
        <li><strong>Why it persists:</strong> a 2024 comparison found collagen fractions of 77% against 75% between two massive-weight-loss groups. Quantity is not the variable.</li>
        <li><strong>Better numbers:</strong> 16 weeks of resistance training raised dermal thickness in middle-aged women; aerobic training did not.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limit:</strong> for moderate or severe excess skin, body-contouring surgery is the only thing that removes it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

