    <div class="jr-prose">

      <h2 id="why-loose">Why does skin stay loose after weight loss?</h2>
      <p>Because it is structural damage to the skin's scaffolding, not a shortage of collagen in your diet. Skin stretched for years and then emptied has to retract, and the fibres that do the retracting come out of the process worst.</p>
      <p>Biopsies make this concrete. A prospective cohort in <em>Obesity Surgery</em> (2023) sampled skin before bariatric surgery and a year after, and found disorganised collagen and fragmented elastic fibres. A 2024 comparison in the same journal measured 80 biopsies from body-contouring patients and reported total collagen fractions of 77% and 75% between its two groups. If loose skin were a collagen deficit, that number would separate loose from tight. It does not.</p>
      <p>Elastin is the harder problem. A review in the <em>International Journal of Cosmetic Science</em> (2010) describes elastin synthesis as repressed in most adult tissues, with mature elastin extremely stable and a half-life estimated near 70 years. Adult skin keeps the elastic fibres it has rather than making new ones, and no oral supplement supplies elastin.</p>

