      <h2 id="does-collagen-work">Does collagen tighten loose skin?</h2>
      <p>There is no trial that answers this, and the absence is the answer. Searching for oral collagen in post-weight-loss skin laxity returns general skin-ageing trials in healthy adults and nothing in people with excess skin. Any page claiming collagen tightens loose skin is extrapolating from a hydration endpoint, not reporting a result.</p>
      <p>What they have been tested for is narrower. A 12-week randomised, placebo-controlled trial of low-molecular-weight collagen peptide improved hydration, elasticity and wrinkling (<em>Nutrients</em>, 2018), and a 2023 meta-analysis of 26 trials across 1,721 participants found a significant elasticity benefit against placebo. Those measure skin quality in intact skin, not how much skin is left over after losing 30 kilos. The <a href="/pages/how-much-collagen-per-day">doses those trials used</a> are worth knowing before you buy.</p>
      <p>The inconvenient part deserves equal space. A 2025 review in <em>The American Journal of Medicine</em> pooled 23 trials across 1,474 participants: collagen improved hydration, elasticity and wrinkles overall, but split by funding, the trials not funded by industry showed no effect. A letter in the same journal disputes that split, arguing five of the seven trials classed as non-funded should have counted as industry-sponsored. Anyone deciding how to spend money should know this effect is contested rather than settled.</p>

