      <h2 id="what-works">What actually helps, in order of evidence</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Lift something heavy, three times a week.</strong> In a 2023 <em>Scientific Reports</em> trial, 61 sedentary middle-aged women trained for 16 weeks. Both kinds of training improved elasticity; only resistance training raised dermal thickness. Best-evidenced item here, and free.</li>
        <li><strong>Eat enough complete protein while losing.</strong> A meta-regression in energy-restricted, resistance-trained people found a broadly linear benefit of higher protein for fat-free mass. More muscle under the skin changes how the skin sits.</li>
        <li><strong>Judge it only at a stable weight.</strong> Plastic surgery units want six to twelve months of weight stability before contouring, because skin and weight are both still moving.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect the elastic fibres you still have.</strong> Daily sunscreen is the best-evidenced way to prevent further elastic-fibre damage, and India's UV load makes that a year-round job. More in <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">how to boost collagen naturally</a>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="india">Rapid weight loss and facial hollowing</h2>
      <p>The newest version of this question in India is about the face, not the abdomen. Since GLP-1 weight-loss injections became widely available here, including the pre-wedding use Indian papers have reported, people are losing a tenth of their body weight far faster than dieting would manage, and arriving in clinic asking about a hollow, deflated face.</p>
      <p>That change is lost fat volume with skin that has not contracted to match it, a consequence of how fast and how much weight came off rather than a drug effect on skin, and no oral supplement replaces fat volume. If you are on a prescribed injection, the useful conversation is with the doctor prescribing it: rate of loss, protein intake, and training to protect lean mass. Contour is a question for a dermatologist or plastic surgeon.</p>

