      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen tighten loose skin after weight loss?</summary>
          <p>No. There is no randomised trial of an oral collagen supplement for loose skin after weight loss, so a brand promising tightening is going past its evidence. Collagen trials measure hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth, not the amount of excess skin. Loose skin involves fragmented elastic fibres, and collagen does not supply elastin.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>If I take collagen while losing weight, will it stop loose skin forming?</summary>
          <p>Nobody has tested that, so the honest answer is unknown. What does predict loose skin is how much weight is lost, how fast, how long it was carried and your age. Collagen during a deficit is not harmful, but it should not displace resistance training and complete protein, which have better evidence.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help facial hollowing after rapid weight loss?</summary>
          <p>Not in the way it is marketed. The facial change after fast weight loss is lost fat volume with skin that has not contracted to match, and no oral supplement replaces fat volume. Collagen peptides act on hydration and elasticity, a different outcome from contour. Facial volume is a question for a dermatologist.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is the 8g of protein in a collagen shot enough to protect my muscle while dieting?</summary>
          <p>No. Collagen is an incomplete protein: it lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, the amino acid that drives muscle protein synthesis, and whey beats it head to head. Count a collagen shot as collagen, and get your lean-mass protein from complete sources such as dairy, eggs, fish, soya or whey.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long should I wait to see whether loose skin retracts on its own?</summary>
          <p>There is no trial timeline, but surgical practice gives a proxy: plastic surgery units generally ask for six to twelve months of stable weight before considering contouring, because skin and weight are both still changing before then. If skin is still loose after a year at a stable weight, large further retraction is unlikely.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is it safe to take collagen alongside a prescribed weight-loss injection?</summary>
          <p>There is no published interaction data either way, which is why it is a question for the prescribing doctor. Collagen peptides are a food protein and generally well tolerated, but GLP-1 medicines commonly cause nausea and reduced appetite, so a flavoured shot may not sit comfortably. Avoid marine collagen with a fish or shellfish allergy.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

