        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is the 8g of protein in a collagen shot enough to protect my muscle while dieting?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Collagen is an incomplete protein: it lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, the amino acid that drives muscle protein synthesis, and whey beats it head to head. Count a collagen shot as collagen, and get your lean-mass protein from complete sources such as dairy, eggs, fish, soya or whey." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How long should I wait to see whether loose skin retracts on its own?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "There is no trial timeline, but surgical practice gives a proxy: plastic surgery units generally ask for six to twelve months of stable weight before considering contouring, because skin and weight are both still changing before then. If skin is still loose after a year at a stable weight, large further retraction is unlikely." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is it safe to take collagen alongside a prescribed weight-loss injection?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "There is no published interaction data either way, which is why it is a question for the prescribing doctor. Collagen peptides are a food protein and generally well tolerated, but GLP-1 medicines commonly cause nausea and reduced appetite, so a flavoured shot may not sit comfortably. Avoid marine collagen with a fish or shellfish allergy." } }
